// Whodunit: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== whodunit.f01 | feature | three-act
title: The Gold-Weigher Of Cairo
genre: whodunit + historical-fiction
tone: Dusty, urbane and exact
era: 1324 | period
setting: A caravanserai in Mamluk Cairo | real | city
logline: When the man who weighs out an African emperor's gold is found poisoned in a locked counting room, the caravan's youngest scribe has three days to name the killer before the pilgrimage moves on and takes every suspect with it.
premise: An emperor from beyond the desert is passing through Cairo on his way to Mecca with a hundred camel-loads of gold, giving it away so freely that the price of gold in the city is falling by the day. All of it passes over one man's scales. On the fourth morning he is dead beside them, door barred from inside, and the weights are wrong.
synopsis: {kanku} keeps the caravan's daybook because she writes a faster hand than her brothers; the court's chief secretary {fode} sets her to find out what happened before the Cairenes do. The first act is the room: a barred door, a high grille, a cup, a balance with one pan heavier than its weights say. The dead man, {bakari}, was honest by all accounts, which in a counting room is a motive. The second act is the suspects, each questioned over a different trade. A Cairo money-changer, {ibn_zaki}, is being ruined by the falling price and has been buying dinars with borrowed silver. The emperor's steward, {sanou}, has been giving in his master's name and keeping no receipts. A Venetian factor wants the secret of where the gold comes from. {kanku} proceeds as a scribe would: she reconciles. The daybook, the dead man's tally and the scales do not agree, and the difference is too regular for theft. {bakari} had found that the weights had been shaved, so that every gift the emperor made was a little short and the difference went somewhere. He had recorded it in the one place nobody reads, the margin sums. The locked room is the least of it; the cup was poisoned an hour before he barred the door himself to count in peace. In the third act she assembles them in the counting room on the last night and lets the balance speak: true weights in one pan, the caravan's in the other. The killer is the man who made the weights and has kept them since Timbuktu. {fode} himself, who chose her for the inquiry because he took a girl for a formality. He is handed to the qadi with his sums. The emperor orders the short gifts made good, which lowers the price of gold further. {kanku} is given the scales.
question: In a world awash with gold, who notices a grain?
themes: honesty as a hazard; reckoning as detection; the underestimated clerk
audience: Adults who like a fair-play puzzle
viewpoint: With {kanku}; every clue is something she writes down, and the audience sees the page
stakes: An emperor's good name in the greatest city of the age, and a scribe who has been set up to fail
conflict: A junior scribe's arithmetic against her own superior, who commissioned the inquiry he expected her to botch
ending: The secretary who shaved the weights and poisoned the weigher is exposed by the balance. The scribe inherits the scales.
experience: The satisfaction of sums that finally agree, a culprit hiding in plain sight, and a very good last line
look: Shafts of light through a stone grille, scales and gold dust, indigo robes against ochre walls
sound: Scales settling, a reed pen, the call to prayer over rooftops. Camels complaining in the yard.
m: expose | secret | loyalty-vs-truth | triumph
@ kanku | Kanku Diabate | lead | protagonist | human | she/her | 18 | To make the book balance | An unbalanced book offends her | She has been chosen because she is expected to fail | Exact, dry, braver on paper than aloud | From copyist to the keeper of the scales | Quiet, precise, quotes figures | Indigo wrap, reed pens in her hair, ink-stained fingertips
@ fode | Fode Sisoko | co-lead | antagonist | human | he/him | 54 | The matter closed before the caravan leaves | He has skimmed since Timbuktu | He appointed the one person who checks margins | Urbane, fatherly, condescending | From patron of the inquiry to its answer | Honeyed, proverbial | White robes, a seal ring, a set of brass weights at his belt
@ ibn_zaki | Yusuf ibn Zaki | supporting | foil | human | he/him | 47 | The price of gold to stop falling | He is ruined if it does not | He was seen at the caravanserai that night | Shrewd, desperate, honest by his own lights | From prime suspect to the witness on Cairo prices | Fast, mercantile, swears by his beard | Striped robe, an abacus of beads
@ sanou | Sanou | supporting | foil | human | he/him | 39 | His master seen as generous | Generosity is policy | He keeps no receipts | Grand, careless, loyal | From suspect to chastened | Booming, formal | Embroidered tunic, a purse always open
@ bakari | Bakari | featured | none | human | he/him | 60 | True weight | He has weighed for three kings | He found a grain missing in every measure | Silent, incorruptible, alone | Seen alive only in the first scene | Speaks in numbers | Plain cotton, a balance in a leather case
~ kanku - fode | scribe and chief secretary | his favour | his exposure
~ kanku - bakari | young scribe and old weigher | a nod across the counting room | his margin sums in her hand
~ kanku - ibn_zaki | investigator and suspect | accusation | he lends her his own true weights
# The Room | INT | Caravanserai, Counting Room | NIGHT | bakari, kanku | See him alive | {bakari} weighs a gift twice, frowns, writes something small in a margin. {kanku}, copying, notices and is waved away | | 3 | props=balance+gold dust+daybook
# The Room | INT | Caravanserai, Counting Room | DAWN | kanku, fode, sanou | Find him | A barred door forced. The weigher by his scales, a cup, a balance that does not sit level. {fode} gives the inquiry to the girl with the neat hand | {bakari} is dead | 5 | props=balance+cup
# The Reckoning | INT | Money-Changers' Arcade | DAY | kanku, ibn_zaki | Question Cairo | {ibn_zaki} shows her his ruin on an abacus: the emperor's alms have cut gold by a fifth. Yes, he was there. To beg | | 5 | crowd=50, props=abacus+coins
# The Reckoning | EXT | Caravanserai, Courtyard | DAY | kanku, sanou | Question the steward | A hundred gifts and no receipts. {sanou} is offended to be asked. She copies his list anyway | | 4 | crowd=40, animals=camels
# The Reckoning | INT | Scribes' Loft | NIGHT | kanku | Reconcile | Three records that do not agree, and a difference that is always the same fraction. In {bakari}'s margins, a column of tiny sums | The weights were shaved | 6 | props=daybook+tally+lamp
# The Balance | INT | Caravanserai, Counting Room | NIGHT | kanku, fode, ibn_zaki, sanou | Let it speak | Everyone assembled. {ibn_zaki}'s weights in one pan, the caravan's in the other. The beam tips. She asks who has carried the caravan's weights since Timbuktu | {fode} is named | 7 | crowd=12, props=balance+two sets of weights
# The Balance | EXT | Caravanserai, Gate | DAWN | kanku, sanou | Take the scales | The caravan forms up for Mecca. {sanou} hands her the leather case with both hands | | 3 | crowd=100, animals=camels
review: authored | 2026-11-12 | written whole; drawn from the Malian pilgrimage caravan in Cairo, with invented people
